WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.900
Long-read sequencing resolves 32 of 46 unexplained cancer genomes that short-read tests had missed. This is Planetterrian Daily, episode 224. John's Hopkins maps a new prostate cell type. USC tracks mitochondrial microproteins in diet, and Nature Neuroscience isolates a psychedelic signaling path, with a science deep dive later. Michigan's Mianco Seek program launched in 2010 as the first national effort to sequence patient tumors by analyzing both

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:36.120
DNA and RNA from normal tissue and tumors. Researchers applied long-read nanopore sequencing,

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:40.560
capable of reading thousands to tens of thousands of letters in one stretch,

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:47.720
to 768 patients with prostate, breast, or ovarian cancer, according to a report in the Good Men project.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:53.080
Conventional short-read sequencing examines only a few hundred DNA letters at a time,

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:58.460
and often fails to show how mutations are arranged or whether both copies of a protective gene

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:02.320
have been disabled through complex rearrangements and chemical modifications.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:11.840
In a broader analysis of 4,496 tumor samples from 3,688 patients, the team used nanopore sequencing

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:19.060
on selected cases to map mutation arrangements in genes including notch 1, PDGF RB, kit

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:20.480
and PI-3CA.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:25.760
One woman with a strong family history of breast and ovarian cancer had tested negative

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:31.280
on a commercial multi-jean panel, yet nanopore sequencing of her blood sample revealed a disease

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:37.560
causing DNA insertion in BRCA2. A separate report in the New England Journal of Medicine

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:43.100
traced a rare spinal tumor in a three-year-old boy, 26 months after gene therapy for

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:50.040
crabby disease, to a fragment of modified adenoe associated virus, or AAV, DNA that inserted

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:53.380
into the GLI-1 gene and drove its overexpression.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:58.600
A rural Chenayan stated that identifying these mutations can help clinicians understand

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:03.200
why certain patients develop drug resistance and how to make better treatment choices,

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:08.760
and that understanding complications like the GLI-1 insertion can guide safer gene therapy

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:09.280
design.

00:02:10.055 --> 00:02:15.215
With one positive basal cell, sit packed around the urethra in the regions where age-related

00:02:15.215 --> 00:02:19.955
prostate growth begins, according to Johns Hopkins researchers in a report on the

00:02:19.955 --> 00:02:24.775
science subreddit. The team identified this previously unknown cell type in the exact

00:02:24.775 --> 00:02:29.735
zones linked to enlargement. The cells are proposed to explain the slow, interrupted

00:02:29.735 --> 00:02:35.395
urination many men experience after age 50. Their location suggests they may respond to

00:02:35.395 --> 00:02:41.154
local mechanical or hormonal signals that accumulate with age. The finding offers a potential

00:02:41.154 --> 00:02:46.594
cellular target for more localized therapies. If the cells prove causal, treatments could

00:02:46.594 --> 00:02:50.814
shift from broad hormonal suppression toward interventions that spare other tissues.

00:02:51.434 --> 00:02:56.074
What remains unknown is whether these cells change in number or activity in men who never

00:02:56.074 --> 00:03:01.094
develop symptomatic enlargement, or whether they interact with known inflammatory pathways

00:03:01.094 --> 00:03:01.854
in the prostate.

00:03:02.925 --> 00:03:07.605
Older adults whose eating habits most closely matched the Mediterranean diet had significantly

00:03:07.605 --> 00:03:12.185
higher concentrations of the mitochondrial microproteins, humanin, and schmousse.

00:03:12.865 --> 00:03:14.945
USC Leonard Davis researchers found.

00:03:14.945 --> 00:03:15.065
Sound.

00:03:15.545 --> 00:03:20.105
Science Daily reported that the team examined blood samples from older adults with varying

00:03:20.105 --> 00:03:21.645
degrees of diet adherence.

00:03:22.125 --> 00:03:26.745
Higher consumption of olive oil, fish, and legumes tracked with increased humanin levels.

00:03:27.385 --> 00:03:32.105
Olive oil intake combined with lower refined carbohydrate consumption tracked with higher

00:03:32.105 --> 00:03:32.885
schmousse levels.

00:03:33.505 --> 00:03:38.065
Participants with stronger diet adherence also displayed lower indicators of oxidative

00:03:38.065 --> 00:03:38.445
stress.

00:03:38.965 --> 00:03:44.205
The process in which reactive oxygen species overwhelm cellular defenses and damage proteins

00:03:44.205 --> 00:03:50.165
and DNA. Roberto Vichinanza noted that these microproteins may act as molecular messengers

00:03:50.165 --> 00:03:53.845
that translate what people eat into how their cells function in age.

00:03:54.365 --> 00:03:59.045
Pinchas Cohen, the study's senior author, added that humanin and Schmuss could serve as

00:03:59.045 --> 00:04:03.585
biomarkers for adherence to the Mediterranean diet and hold clinical significance.

00:04:04.799 --> 00:04:08.799
Psychedelics can sustain antidepressant-like effects after the drug has cleared,

00:04:09.259 --> 00:04:13.379
and a Nature Neuroscience study mapped the intracellular pathway that allows it,

00:04:13.379 --> 00:04:16.039
according to a report on the science subreddit.

00:04:16.419 --> 00:04:18.799
The work isolates beta arrest and recruitment

00:04:18.799 --> 00:04:21.979
at the 5HT2A receptor as a key mechanism.

00:04:22.399 --> 00:04:24.239
That pathway operates separately

00:04:24.239 --> 00:04:26.259
from classical G-protein signaling.

00:04:27.378 --> 00:04:28.918
Rolling snakes keep their head steady

00:04:28.918 --> 00:04:30.478
relative to the body axis

00:04:30.478 --> 00:04:33.438
through specific kinematic adjustments during locomotion.

00:04:34.118 --> 00:04:36.458
Researchers documented the locomotor patterns

00:04:36.458 --> 00:04:37.698
and head movement control

00:04:37.698 --> 00:04:40.498
that maintained visual stability during the rolling gate.

00:04:40.498 --> 00:04:47.186
Seabird ancestry predicted plastic accumulation better than diet, feeding method, or body size,

00:04:47.566 --> 00:04:55.246
an analysis of 93,281 seabirds from 240 species recorded plastic in 182 species.

00:04:55.726 --> 00:04:59.746
Phylogenetic relationships can help identify seabirds at elevated risk.

00:05:00.663 --> 00:05:05.943
Older adults who napped most days or every day carried a 48% elevated risk of developing

00:05:05.943 --> 00:05:08.783
dementia compared with rare or never-nappers.

00:05:08.783 --> 00:05:14.343
The association held after adjustment for demographics, health conditions, and nighttime sleep.

00:05:14.783 --> 00:05:17.563
It persisted across multiple sensitivity analyses.

00:05:18.586 --> 00:05:23.446
Reduced sugar intake in early life linked to modestly slower brain aging and lower dementia

00:05:23.446 --> 00:05:24.966
incidents decades later.

00:05:25.466 --> 00:05:29.586
A 15-year follow-up track participants from childhood into later adulthood.

00:05:30.046 --> 00:05:35.386
Ultra-process foods carried a 12% higher likelihood of digestive cancer in cohort studies,

00:05:35.386 --> 00:05:39.146
and a 42% higher likelihood in case control studies.

00:05:39.626 --> 00:05:41.786
Ultra-process meat and protein products

00:05:41.786 --> 00:05:43.706
showed the strongest subcategory association

00:05:44.146 --> 00:05:46.206
at 33% elevated risk.

00:05:46.826 --> 00:05:48.506
The meta-analysis combined results

00:05:48.506 --> 00:05:50.186
across multiple populations.

00:05:51.301 --> 00:05:53.281
Acuprate superconductor long assumed

00:05:53.281 --> 00:05:55.121
to have uniform structure actually

00:05:55.121 --> 00:05:57.821
contains regions of two different atomic arrangements

00:05:57.821 --> 00:06:00.841
separated by unusually wide boundary zones.

00:06:01.381 --> 00:06:03.081
University of Warwick researchers

00:06:03.081 --> 00:06:08.521
users scanning three-dimensional X-ray diffraction at the European Synchrotron radiation facility,

00:06:08.961 --> 00:06:13.941
according to Science Daily, in a result that challenges a 40-year assumption about these crystals.

00:06:14.521 --> 00:06:19.021
The boundaries are hundreds of times broader than expected, and may actively interfere

00:06:19.021 --> 00:06:22.301
with superconductivity rather than simply co-existing with it.

00:06:22.841 --> 00:06:27.361
Mark Senn said the finding means existing bulk measurements and theoretical models will

00:06:27.361 --> 00:06:31.861
need reinterpretation because the crystal is fundamentally patchworked and textured.

00:06:31.861 --> 00:06:36.661
The team suspects similar hidden structural patterns may be widespread among other

00:06:36.661 --> 00:06:41.061
coupe-rate superconductors and related materials studied under extreme pressure.

00:06:41.701 --> 00:06:46.701
The measurements were made possible by a recent 150 million euro upgrade to the synchrotron

00:06:46.701 --> 00:06:50.841
that provides extremely intense x-ray beams for studying materials in detail.

00:06:51.876 --> 00:06:56.716
In brief, scientists writing in Science Daily proposed that polarized light detection paired

00:06:56.716 --> 00:07:01.496
with specially designed fluorescent molecules could distinguish hypothetical mirror image

00:07:01.496 --> 00:07:05.836
organisms from ordinary life if such organisms were ever synthesized.

00:07:06.296 --> 00:07:11.936
A single x-ray pulse imaging method reported by Fizz.org captures instabilities and

00:07:11.936 --> 00:07:17.816
turbulent structures in plasmas ranging from nuclear fusion to laser plasma regimes in one exposure.

00:07:18.829 --> 00:07:23.409
Something most people get wrong about the Mediterranean diet is that its health effects come only

00:07:23.409 --> 00:07:26.929
from antioxidants or from changes in blood lipids and inflammation.

00:07:27.489 --> 00:07:31.969
An additional route runs through two tiny proteins produced inside mitochondria.

00:07:32.649 --> 00:07:38.269
Humanin and SHMOS originate from short open-reading frames within mitochondrial DNA.

00:07:39.029 --> 00:07:41.529
Those segments were once considered non-functional.

00:07:41.909 --> 00:07:44.989
They are now known to encode biologically active molecules.

00:07:46.098 --> 00:07:50.438
Humanin was first identified in 2003 by Pinchus, Cohen, and colleagues.

00:07:50.438 --> 00:07:57.238
That identification sits inside more than 20 years of work on small proteins encoded by mitochondrial DNA.

00:07:57.998 --> 00:08:01.018
Later work linked humanin to improved insulin sensitivity.

00:08:01.758 --> 00:08:08.458
It has also been tied to better cardiovascular outcomes, increased longevity and protection against cognitive decline.

00:08:09.338 --> 00:08:11.658
Mitochondria generate most of the cell's energy.

00:08:12.098 --> 00:08:15.278
They also regulate stress responses and aging processes.

00:08:15.278 --> 00:08:20.738
The microproteins appear to function as internal messengers that convert dietary information

00:08:20.738 --> 00:08:22.998
into adjustments in mitochondrial output.

00:08:23.698 --> 00:08:29.438
Clinicians might one day monitor humanin or SHMS levels to assess whether dietary interventions

00:08:29.438 --> 00:08:34.658
are producing the expected cellular effects rather than relying solely on self-reported

00:08:34.658 --> 00:08:35.218
food logs.

00:08:35.858 --> 00:08:40.318
Open questions include whether other eating patterns or lifestyle factors influence the

00:08:40.318 --> 00:08:41.738
same mitochondrial signals.

00:08:41.738 --> 00:08:48.638
Another is whether genetic variation in mitochondrial DNA affects an individual's response to the Mediterranean diet.

00:08:49.338 --> 00:08:55.038
Looking back over the past seven days, daily temperature swings, single-protein tracking and living neurons,

00:08:55.538 --> 00:08:58.598
and virus host genetic conflicts set the weeks through line.

00:08:59.178 --> 00:09:04.178
Daily weather swings were shown to accelerate glacier melt more than monthly averages had indicated.

00:09:04.778 --> 00:09:09.818
A nanoscale platform now tracks single proteins moving inside living neurons in real time.

00:09:09.818 --> 00:09:15.298
Genetic conflicts between viruses and hosts were shown to shape rapid evolution across species.

00:09:15.818 --> 00:09:20.858
Before we go, longer trials could test whether deliberately raising humanin or schmousse through

00:09:20.858 --> 00:09:26.178
targeted diet changes or other means, slows measurable declines in heart or brain function.

00:09:26.638 --> 00:09:28.778
That covers today's science and health news.

